WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language:  en

00:00:00.079 --> 00:00:03.780
66 million years ago an asteroid longer

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:06.980
than Mount Everest is tall hurtled toward the Earth

00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:08.840
crashing into the Gulf of Mexico.

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:12.760
More than 75% of plant and animal species,

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:15.280
including the dinosaurs, vanished.

00:00:15.289 --> 00:00:18.680
it took over 4 million years for life to

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:21.090
flourish once again, but the environment

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:22.720
was altered forever.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:25.980
So scientists were shocked when they discovered

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:29.220
another large impact crater, buried a kilometer

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:31.680
under Greenland's Hiawatha glacier.

00:00:32.369 --> 00:00:34.320
What was even more shocking was the crater's

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:36.300
lack of erosion, which suggests that the

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:39.120
asteroid could have made impact when humans were

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:40.400
roaming the earth.

00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:43.680
To be certain that their find was really an

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:47.340
impact crater, researchers used ice penetrating radar

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:49.440
to get a sense of the shape of the bedrock

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:51.240
underneath the ice.

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:54.640
The radar revealed one tell-tale characteristic:

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:57.580
a raised area in the center, where the ground would have

00:00:57.580 --> 00:00:59.480
rebounded after the asteroid hit,

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:01.880
like water struck by a stone.

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:05.100
Further evidence came from the sediment draining

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:08.140
out of the glacier itself. Shocked quartz grains

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:09.860
were discovered, which could only be

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:13.760
formed by extraterrestrial strikes or nuclear weapons.

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:17.580
It was, indeed, an impact crater.

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:20.000
31 kilometers in diameter

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:22.530
that makes it one of the 25 largest known

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:24.640
craters in the world, though it is

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:26.280
smaller than the asteroid impact that

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:29.700
killed the dinosaurs, and the Chesapeake Bay crater.

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:32.000
The asteroid making the Hiawatha crater would have

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:35.220
needed to be enormous too, with a diameter of more

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:38.000
than 1.5 kilometers.

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:43.060
Scientists still aren't sure how young Hiawatha is, but it is

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:45.400
new enough that it is the only crater of its

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:47.200
size still visible to the naked eye--

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:50.742
at least, if you could see through the ice.

00:01:50.742 --> 00:01:53.460
Evidence suggests it is between a

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:57.960
100,000 to 12,000 years old, though it could also be

00:01:57.960 --> 00:01:59.740
millions of years older.

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:02.780
If the impact happened when Greenland was covered in

00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:05.880
ice, which is most of the past two million years,

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:08.760
billions of tons of ice would have vaporized in an

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:11.580
instant, leading to an influx of fresh water into the

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:12.980
world's oceans.

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:16.480
Just such an influx  may have happened about

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:20.159
12,800 years ago, helping to set off a thousand-year

00:02:20.159 --> 00:02:23.060
glacial period called the Younger Dryas.

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:26.930
But evidence for such a flood remains inconclusive.

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:30.980
When the asteroid did hit, faraway regions would have

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:32.120
felt the impact.

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:35.200
Hurricane-force winds would have engulfed the region

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:38.140
and objects as far away as a hundred kilometers

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:39.030
would have been leveled.

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:43.000
The next step is pinning down precisely when this

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:44.620
massive asteroid arrived.

00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:47.560
And then the search can begin for other craters

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:50.300
lurking beneath the ice.

